Here are two traditional Ghanaian recipes: Red Red (Black-Eyed Pea Stew) and Oto (Spiced Mashed Yam).

1. Red Red (Ghanaian Bean Stew)

A highly popular Ghanaian dish named for its vibrant color, typically cooked with palm oil or coconut oil and traditionally served with fried sweet plantains.
• Prep time: 20 minutes
• Cook time: 40 minutes
• Servings: 4 people

Ingredients

• 300g black-eyed peas (soaked in water overnight)
• 3 tbsp coconut oil (or traditional red palm oil)
• 3 large tomatoes (blended with Scotch Bonnet peppers)
• 1 medium onion (sliced)
• 2 tbsp dried shrimp powder
• ½ smoked mackerel
• 4 green Scotch Bonnet peppers (or to taste)
• Salt (to taste)

Instructions

1. Cook the beans: Drain the soaked beans, rinse them, and boil in a pot of water until tender.
2. Make the sauce base: Heat the oil in a pan, add the sliced onions, and sauté until translucent. Stir in the blended tomato-pepper mixture and the shrimp powder. Simmer for about 10 minutes.
3. Add the fish: Fold in the flaked smoked mackerel and a pinch of salt, then let it simmer for another 5 minutes.
4. Combine: Add the cooked beans to the sauce, stir gently, and let everything simmer together for an additional 10 minutes.
5. Serve: Serve hot alongside fried ripe plantains or white rice.

2. Oto (Ghanaian Mashed Yam)

A comforting, celebratory dish traditionally prepared to mark special occasions such as weddings, naming ceremonies, or birthdays.
• Prep time: 10 minutes
• Cook time: 20 minutes
• Servings: 3 to 4 people

Ingredients

• 3 large tubers of Ghanaian yam (peeled and cubed)
• ¼ cup red palm oil
• 1 red onion (finely sliced)
• 1 bouillon cube (shrimp or chicken flavor)
• 3 hard-boiled eggs (peeled)
• Sea salt (to taste)

Instructions

1. Boil the yam: Place the yam cubes into a large pot of salted boiling water and cook until fork-tender. Drain the water and mash the yams thoroughly in a large bowl.
2. Infuse the oil: Meanwhile, heat the red palm oil in a small pan over medium heat. Caramelize the sliced onion, then stir in the crumbled bouillon cube until completely dissolved.
3. Mix: Pour the hot oil and onion mixture directly over the mashed yam. Mix thoroughly until the yam absorbs the oil and turns a vibrant yellow-orange color. Season with additional salt if needed.
4. Garnish and serve: Top the mashed yam with the hard-boiled eggs (whole or halved) before serving warm.
